WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.496
You ask the bot to flag gaps in

00:00:02.496 --> 00:00:04.992
a volunteer roster. Eighty shifts later, you discover

00:00:04.992 --> 00:00:07.488
it counted every blank cell as a missing

00:00:07.488 --> 00:00:07.800
volunteer.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.378
Look at these two rows. Check-in needs a

00:00:10.378 --> 00:00:12.956
person. Standby is deliberately empty. The bot can

00:00:12.956 --> 00:00:15.533
repeat a reasonable guess eighty times before you

00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:16.500
see it once.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.071
Try a smaller instruction. Check the first three

00:00:19.071 --> 00:00:21.643
shifts only. For each gap, show the row

00:00:21.643 --> 00:00:24.214
and your reason. Then wait for my reply

00:00:24.214 --> 00:00:25.500
before scanning the rest.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.532
Now the mistake costs one correction. The bot

00:00:28.532 --> 00:00:31.563
flagged both blanks. You can see exactly which

00:00:31.563 --> 00:00:32.700
rule it inferred.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.456
Reply in the thread. Standby may be empty.

00:00:35.456 --> 00:00:38.211
Run those same three again and keep the

00:00:38.211 --> 00:00:40.967
rest on hold. One flag should survive: the

00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:42.000
empty check-in shift.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.400
Once the sample matches your judgment, give it

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.800
the next batch. If it calls another agent,

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.200
carry the same limit into that handoff. A

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.600
small Slack request can start a much larger

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:51.900
job.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.557
Before your next bulk request, finish this sentence:

00:00:54.557 --> 00:00:57.214
do the first how many, show me what,

00:00:57.214 --> 00:00:59.871
and stop where? Give the bot a checkpoint

00:00:59.871 --> 00:01:01.200
you can actually review.
